.TH "OSTRYA\-CONFIG" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-config \- Read or write a repository configuration value
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya config [OPTIONS] [OPERATION] [ARGS]...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read or write a repository configuration value.
.PP
The command reads and writes the \fB\%config\fR file of the repository. \fB\%get\fR prints the value and a newline. \fB\%set\fR and \fB\%unset\fR print nothing. A missing group or key is an error for \fB\%get\fR\&.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIOPERATION\fR
The operation: \fB\%get\fR, \fB\%set\fR, or \fB\%unset\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%get\fR prints the value. \fB\%set\fR writes the value, and creates the group if it does not exist. \fB\%unset\fR removes the key. If the key or its group does not exist, \fB\%unset\fR changes nothing and exits \%0. \fB\%set\fR and \fB\%unset\fR write the whole \fB\%config\fR file of the repository again. This write drops the comment lines and the blank lines of the file. \fB\%set\fR and \fB\%unset\fR refuse a key of the group \fB\%remote "NAME"\fR when NAME is a remote of the remotes directory.
.TP
\fIARGS...\fR
The key to read or write, and for \fB\%set\fR the value.
.IP
The key is \fB\%section.key\fR, split on the first \fB\%\&.\fR, or a bare key name with \%\-\-group. The command refuses more operands than the operation takes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-group\fR \fIGROUP\fR
The group of KEY. With this option, KEY is a bare key name.
.IP
The key name can then hold \fB\%\&.\fR characters.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The file \fB\%<repo>/config\fR is a key file.
\fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5) gives its place in the repository.
.IP \(bu 2
A line \fB\%[GROUP]\fR starts a group.
A line \fB\%KEY=VALUE\fR sets a key in the current group.
A line that starts with \fB\%#\fR is a comment.
.IP \(bu 2
The key ends at the first \fB\%=\fR.
Space and tab around the key, and at the start of the value, are removed.
A repeated group adds to the first group, and a repeated key takes the last value.
.IP \(bu 2
A boolean is \fB\%true\fR, \fB\%false\fR, \fB\%1\fR, or \fB\%0\fR, with no other bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
An integer is a whole decimal number, with an optional \fB\%\-\fR sign.
A value with a \fB\%+\fR sign is refused.
.IP \(bu 2
A list is a series of items, each followed by \fB\%;\fR.
The last \fB\%;\fR is optional.
\fB\%\e;\fR is a \fB\%;\fR in an item.
.IP \(bu 2
In a string, \fB\%\es\fR is a space, \fB\%\et\fR a tab, \fB\%\en\fR a newline, \fB\%\er\fR a carriage return, and \fB\%\e\e\fR a backslash.
\fB\%ostrya config set\fR writes these escapes, and \fB\%ostrya config get\fR prints the value without them.
.PP
ostrya reads at most 1048576 bytes of \fB\%config\fR, and refuses a larger file.
ostrya checks \fB\%repo_version\fR, \fB\%mode\fR, and \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR when it opens the repository.
It reads each other key when a command needs it, and a malformed value makes that command fail.
\fB\%ostrya config set\fR does not check the group, the key, or the value.
.PP
ostrya keeps each group and each key that it does not know when it writes \fB\%config\fR.
A write removes the comments and the blank lines.
.SH CORE KEYS
The keys of the group \fB\%[core]\fR.
\fB\%ostrya init\fR writes \fB\%repo_version\fR, \fB\%mode\fR, and \fB\%collection\-id\fR.
.TP
\fB\%repo_version\fR (integer)
The version of the repository format.
It must be 1.
ostrya refuses to open a repository without it, or with another value.
.TP
\fB\%mode\fR (string)
The mode of the repository: \fB\%bare\fR, \fB\%bare\-user\fR, \fB\%bare\-user\-only\fR, \fB\%bare\-split\-xattrs\fR, \fB\%archive\-z2\fR, \fB\%archive\fR, or \fB\%bare\-user\-shared\fR.
\fB\%archive\fR is a second name of \fB\%archive\-z2\fR.
\fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5) describes each mode.
ostrya refuses to open a repository without this key, or with another value.
.TP
\fB\%collection\-id\fR (string)
The collection ID of the repository.
The summary records it, and the collection refs below \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR use it.
The refs of this id are the local refs below \fB\%refs/heads/\fR.
ostrya writes no ref below \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR of this id.
The resolve, the listing of \fB\%refs \-c\fR, the ref listing, and the summary ignore that directory.
\fB\%ostrya fsck\fR reports each ref in it, and \fB\%ostrya prune\fR keeps the commit of each ref in it.
\fB\%ostrya init \-\-collection\-id\fR writes it.
In the \fB\%config\fR of a bucket repository, \fB\%ostrya remote init \-\-collection\-id\fR writes it, and it never changes.
\fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5) describes the \fB\%config\fR of a bucket repository.
.TP
\fB\%fsync\fR (boolean, default true)
Sync each write to disk before it completes.
\fB\%ostrya commit \-\-fsync=false\fR, and \fB\%\-\-disable\-fsync\fR of \fB\%ostrya pull\fR and \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR, turn the sync off for one command.
.TP
\fB\%per\-object\-fsync\fR (boolean, default false)
Sync each content object when it is staged.
\fB\%\-\-per\-object\-fsync\fR of \fB\%ostrya pull\fR and \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR turns it on for one command.
.TP
\fB\%locking\fR (boolean, default true)
Take the repository lock \fB\%.lock\fR.
The update lock \fB\%.update.lock\fR is always taken.
A push session of \fB\%ostrya receive\fR or \fB\%ostrya serve\fR refuses a repository with \fB\%locking=false\fR.
\fB\%ostrya prune\fR writes a warning when it is false.
.TP
\fB\%lock\-timeout\-secs\fR (integer, default 300)
The time in seconds that a command waits for a lock.
\fB\%\-1\fR means no limit.
A value less than \fB\%\-1\fR is refused.
.TP
\fB\%tmp\-expiry\-secs\fR (integer, default 86400)
The age in seconds after which a transaction removes a temporary entry of \fB\%tmp/\fR.
A transaction removes a staging directory whose lock is free at any age.
.TP
\fB\%min\-free\-space\-size\fR (string)
The free space that a transaction must leave on the file system.
The value is a number followed by \fB\%MB\fR, \fB\%GB\fR, or \fB\%TB\fR.
\fB\%MB\fR is 1048576 bytes, \fB\%GB\fR is 1024 \fB\%MB\fR, and \fB\%TB\fR is 1024 \fB\%GB\fR.
Any other form is refused, for example \fB\%1gb\fR, \fB\%1.5GB\fR, or a value with a trailing space.
When this key is set, \fB\%min\-free\-space\-percent\fR has no effect.
.TP
\fB\%min\-free\-space\-percent\fR (integer, default 3)
The free space that a transaction must leave, as a percentage of the file system size.
A value outside 0 to 100 is refused.
.TP
\fB\%tombstone\-commits\fR (boolean, default false)
Write a \fB\%.tombstone\-commit\fR object for each commit that \fB\%ostrya prune\fR deletes.
The summary records the value as \fB\%ostree.summary.tombstone\-commits\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR records the value of the \fB\%config\fR of a bucket repository in its summary.
\fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR writes no tombstone, so it refuses a bucket repository whose \fB\%config\fR sets this key to true.
.TP
\fB\%indexed\-deltas\fR (boolean, default true)
The root summary records the value as \fB\%ostree.summary.indexed\-deltas\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR records the value of the \fB\%config\fR of a bucket repository in its summary.
In an archive repository, the summary of each authentication item also records it, and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR copies the key into the \fB\%config\fR that it serves.
.TP
\fB\%auto\-update\-summary\fR (boolean, default false)
Write each summary again after a push changes a ref, as \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR writes them.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR read it.
\fB\%ostrya commit\fR and \fB\%ostrya pull\fR do not write the summary.
.TP
\fB\%commit\-update\-summary\fR (boolean, default false)
A deprecated name of \fB\%auto\-update\-summary\fR.
If one of the two keys is true, the summary is written.
A value that is not a boolean in one of the two keys is refused.
.TP
\fB\%add\-remotes\-config\-dir\fR (boolean, default true)
Write a new remote of \fB\%ostrya remote add\fR into the remotes directory of the repository.
With false, the add writes the group into \fB\%config\fR.
The key has an effect only for a repository with a remotes directory, and only in \fB\%config\fR.
A value that is not a boolean makes the add fail.
"Remotes directory" under REMOTE KEYS gives the rules of the write.
.SH ARCHIVE KEYS
The keys of the group \fB\%[archive]\fR.
.TP
\fB\%zlib\-level\fR (integer, default 6)
The DEFLATE level of the \fB\%.filez\fR objects that ostrya writes into an archive repository, and that \fB\%ostrya serve\fR makes from another mode.
A value less than 1 gives 1, and a value more than 9 gives 9.
In the \fB\%config\fR of a bucket repository, \fB\%ostrya remote init \-\-zlib\-level\fR writes it.
\fB\%ostrya push\fR and \fB\%ostrya push\-tree\fR to an \fB\%s3://\fR address make each \fB\%.filez\fR at the level of the bucket, and not at the level of the local repository.
\fB\%ostrya push\-tree \-\-compress=LEVEL\fR has precedence over the level of the bucket.
\fB\%ostrya push\fR uploads each \fB\%.filez\fR of a local archive repository as it is stored.
.SH INTEGRITY KEYS
The keys of the group \fB\%[ex\-integrity]\fR.
Each key takes \fB\%no\fR, \fB\%maybe\fR, or \fB\%yes\fR, and ostrya refuses any other value.
.TP
\fB\%fsverity\fR (default no)
Seal each loose object that is a regular file with fs\-verity, in each mode.
With \fB\%maybe\fR, a write continues if the file system cannot seal the object.
With \fB\%yes\fR, the write fails.
If the key is absent and \fB\%composefs\fR is \fB\%maybe\fR or \fB\%yes\fR, the default is \fB\%maybe\fR.
.TP
\fB\%composefs\fR (default no)
Sets the default of \fB\%fsverity\fR.
ostrya reads it for no other purpose.
.SH REMOTE KEYS
The keys of a group \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote add\fR writes the group into \fB\%config\fR, or into a file of the remotes directory.
\fB\%NAME\fR has one or more letters, digits, \fB\%\-\fR, \fB\%_\fR, or \fB\%.\fR, and starts with a letter, a digit, or \fB\%_\fR.
.SS "Remotes directory"
A group \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR can also be in a \fB\%.conf\fR file of the remotes directory of the repository.
The \fB\%ostrya\fR command selects the remotes directory in this order:
.IP 1. 3
the global option \fB\%\-\-remotes\-config\-dir\fR;
.IP 2. 3
the key \fB\%remotes\-config\-dir\fR of \fB\%[ex\-ostrya]\fR;
.IP 3. 3
\fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d\fR, when the repository is the system repository;
.IP 4. 3
no remotes directory.
.PP
The system repository is the directory of \fB\%/ostree/repo\fR, by device and inode.
So a symlink to that directory and a bind mount of it are also the system repository.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool uses \fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d\fR for the system repository alone.
The source repository of \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR and each \fB\%\-\-localcache\-repo\fR take their remotes directory from the key alone.
The ostrya library detects no system repository.
A program that uses the library gives the remotes directory when it opens the repository.
Each open of the repository reads the directory.
.PP
ostrya reads each entry whose name ends in \fB\%.conf\fR, in the byte order of the names.
The match is case-sensitive.
A name that starts with \fB\%.\fR is read.
ostrya follows a symlink.
It skips each entry that is not a regular file after the symlink: a directory, a symlink to an absent file, a FIFO, a socket, or a device.
Each other failure of the symlink, such as a loop or a path through a file, fails the open.
It does not read subdirectories.
An absent directory gives no remotes.
.PP
ostrya takes the \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR groups of a file and ignores each other group.
So a \fB\%[core]\fR key or an \fB\%[ex\-ostrya]\fR key in a \fB\%.conf\fR file has no effect, and a \fB\%trust\fR key of a remote in a file names a trust group of \fB\%config\fR.
Two groups with one name in one file give one remote, and a key of the second group replaces the same key of the first.
.PP
These fail each open of the repository:
a path that is not a directory, a directory or a file that ostrya cannot read, a symlink that ostrya cannot follow for a reason other than an absent target, a file larger than 1 MiB (1048576 bytes), a file name or a file content that is not valid UTF\-8, a file that is not a valid key file, and a remote name that two sources define.
The sources are \fB\%config\fR and each \fB\%.conf\fR file.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool reads a larger file, and fails on a value that is not valid UTF\-8 only when it reads that value.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya remote add\fR writes a new remote into the remotes directory when the repository has one and \fB\%add\-remotes\-config\-dir\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR is not false.
Else it writes the group into \fB\%config\fR.
The add writes the file \fB\%NAME.conf\fR with the one group \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR, at mode 0666 with the umask applied.
If the directory is absent, the add creates it and each absent parent, at mode 0777 with the umask applied.
The add needs search permission on each parent that exists, as \fB\%mkdir \-p\fR does.
With \fB\%fsync\fR on, it also needs read permission on the parent of each directory that it creates, because it syncs that parent.
The add refuses NAME when a source holds the remote, and when the directory holds an entry \fB\%NAME.conf\fR of any type.
\fB\%\-\-if\-not\-exists\fR and \fB\%\-\-force\fR do not change the refusal of such an entry.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool writes over the entry, and the remotes in it are lost.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya remote add \-\-force\fR and \fB\%ostrya remote delete\fR edit the file that holds the remote: \fB\%config\fR or a \fB\%.conf\fR file.
\fB\%\-\-force\fR writes the new group at the end of the file, and the other groups of the file stay.
A delete removes the group.
If the file then holds no remote group, the delete removes the file, with each other group of it.
For a symlink, the delete removes the link, and the target stays.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool writes a \fB\%.conf\fR file again with the new group alone, and removes the whole file of a deleted remote, with each other remote in it.
.PP
A write of a \fB\%.conf\fR file drops its comment lines and its blank lines, and keeps its groups and keys in their order.
ostrya writes a temporary file in the directory, whose name does not end in \fB\%.conf\fR, and renames it over the file.
A rewrite keeps the permission bits of the file, and does not keep its owner.
ostrya refuses to write a \fB\%.conf\fR file that is a symlink, because the rename replaces the link with a regular file.
With \fB\%fsync\fR on, the write syncs the file and the directory before the command completes.
.PP
The update lock of the repository covers each write.
It is the lock of one repository, so two repositories that share one remotes directory do not exclude each other.
Each write changes one entry of the directory, so a race of the two loses at most the edit of one file.
.PP
Each command that edits a remote or \fB\%config\fR reads the directory again under the update lock.
So a \fB\%.conf\fR file that fails the open, and that comes into the directory after the open, makes the edit fail.
.PP
The remotes directory can also hold the GPG keyring \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR of a remote.
ostrya reads it only when the repository holds no \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR.
An empty repository keyring hides it.
A repository keyring that ostrya cannot read fails the verification, and ostrya does not read the keyring of the directory.
A symlink to an absent file is no keyring.
A keyring of the directory that ostrya cannot open or read for a reason other than its absence fails the verification, and the refusal names its path with the words "of the remotes directory".
\fB\%ostrya remote gpg\-list\-keys\fR reads the same keyring.
\fB\%ostrya remote gpg\-import\fR writes \fB\%<repo>/NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR, also for a remote of the directory, and never writes a keyring of the directory.
When no file is at \fB\%<repo>/NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR, \fB\%ostrya \%remote \%gpg\-import\fR and \fB\%ostrya \%remote \%add \%\-\-gpg\-import\fR create it, and that keyring then hides the keyring of the directory.
An import reads that path as a verification does: the read follows a symlink, and a symlink to an absent file is no keyring.
The import writes a temporary file and renames it at that path, so a symlink there becomes a regular file, and the target of the symlink does not change.
The import copies the keys, so a later change to the keyring of the directory has no effect.
To trust the keys of the directory too, import that keyring into the repository keyring, before or after the other keys, with \fB\%ostrya \%remote \%gpg\-import \%\-\-keyring \%PATH \%NAME\fR.
PATH is the keyring of NAME in the remotes directory, and a relative PATH is relative to the current directory.
For \fB\%ostrya \%remote \%add \%\-\-gpg\-import\fR, run this command after the add.
\fB\%ostrya remote delete\fR does not remove a keyring of the directory.
A later remote of the same name trusts that keyring while no file is at \fB\%<repo>/NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya config get\fR, \fB\%ostrya config set\fR, and \fB\%ostrya config unset\fR read and write \fB\%config\fR alone.
\fB\%ostrya config set\fR and \fB\%ostrya config unset\fR refuse a key of the group \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR when NAME is a remote of the directory, and write nothing.
.SS "Address"
.TP
\fB\%url\fR (string)
The base URL of a pull over HTTP: \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR.
When \fB\%pull\-url\fR is set, a pull uses it in place of this key.
When \fB\%push\-url\fR is absent, a push uses this key if it starts with \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR.
A pull refuses an ssh address and an \fB\%s3://\fR address in this key, and a push refuses an \fB\%s3://\fR address in it.
\fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR reads a \fB\%file://\fR address in this key as a local repository, as \fB\%pull\-url\fR states.
.TP
\fB\%branches\fR (list)
The refs that \fB\%ostrya pull NAME\fR fetches when the command names no ref.
.TP
\fB\%collection\-id\fR (string)
The collection that the remote serves.
\fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection ID\fR takes each remote whose \fB\%collection\-id\fR is \fB\%ID\fR as a candidate, in the order of the config.
A pull that names the remote does not read this key.
\fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR reads the key of each remote, so a value with a malformed escape sequence in any remote fails that command.
A candidate whose \fB\%pull\-url\fR is an \fB\%s3://\fR address serves the refs that the summary of its bucket repository lists, and the pull skips such a candidate if its bucket repository has no summary.
A candidate whose pull address is a \fB\%file://\fR address is a local repository, which the pull reads through the file system.
The client groups of that repository do not apply, and the file permissions are the access.
\fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR writes the summary of a bucket repository.
.SS "Verification"
These keys apply to \fB\%ostrya pull NAME\fR and to \fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-remote=NAME\fR.
The checks of the commits apply to each target, each parent that the depth takes, and each commit of a chain of static deltas.
A pull checks no signature of a dependency in field 2 of a commit, unless the dependency also has one of these roles.
When the keys of a \fB\%file://\fR candidate of \fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR check the signatures of the commits or of the summary, the pull hashes each object that it imports from that candidate.
These keys do not make \fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-remote=NAME\fR hash an object.
For a source that is not trusted, give \fB\%\-\-untrusted\fR to \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck NAME\fR reads \fB\%gpg\-verify\-summary\fR, \fB\%sign\-verify\-summary\fR, and the trusted keys of these two checks.
A switch of the command that turns a check on or off wins over its key.
For a remote with the key \fB\%trust\fR, the switches of the checks of the commits follow the rule of that key.
\fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-trust=NAME\fR names a trust group for one pull, and the same rule applies.
When the config has no group for \fB\%NAME\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR makes only the checks that its switches ask for.
\fB\%ostree pull\-local\fR reads none of these keys.
.TP
\fB\%gpg\-verify\fR (boolean, default true)
A pull checks the GPG signatures of its commits.
The trusted keys are in \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR and in the keyring of the remote.
The keyring of the remote is \fB\%<repo>/NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR, or, only when that file is absent, \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR in the remotes directory of the repository.
When neither \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR exists, the global trusted keyrings take part too.
The keyring of the remote and the global keyrings must be binary OpenPGP keyrings.
A pull refuses an armored one: the repository keyring by its name, and each other keyring by its path.
A build without GPG support refuses \fB\%true\fR.
.TP
\fB\%gpg\-verify\-summary\fR (boolean, default false)
A pull checks the GPG signature of the summary in \fB\%summary.sig\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck NAME\fR checks the signature of the summary of a bucket repository with these keys too.
.TP
\fB\%gpgkeypath\fR (list)
More keyring files, or directories of keyring files, that \fB\%gpg\-verify\fR trusts.
A \fB\%;\fR separates the entries.
ostrya reads the raw value and decodes no escape sequence, so \fB\%k\esspace.gpg\fR names the file with that name.
One \fB\%;\fR at the end of the value gives no entry.
Each other empty entry fails the pull, and so does an empty value.
ostrya reads each regular file of a directory as a keyring, with any name.
It does not read a subdirectory or a symlink of the directory.
ostrya skips a file that does not hold the bytes \fB\%\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN PGP\fR and that is a GnuPG keybox or is not a whole OpenPGP packet stream, and the file adds no key.
An entry that does not exist fails the pull.
Each other file that ostrya cannot read whole as a keyring also fails the pull.
Examples are a file that ostrya cannot open, a file larger than 4 MiB, a keyring with more than 256 certificates, armor that does not decode to a keyring, and a packet stream that the parser refuses.
\fB\%ostrya show \-\-gpg\-verify\-remote=NAME\fR also trusts these entries, and an entry that does not exist or is empty fails its signature report.
.TP
\fB\%sign\-verify\fR (boolean or list, default false)
A pull checks the signatures of its commits with the signature engines.
\fB\%true\fR uses each engine that has a key.
A list names the engines, split on \fB\%,\fR or \fB\%;\fR, and each engine must have a key.
The engines are \fB\%ed25519\fR, and \fB\%spki\fR in a build with SPKI support.
The keys are in the two keys below and in the system key stores of \fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5).
.TP
\fB\%sign\-verify\-summary\fR (boolean or list, default false)
A pull checks the signature of the summary with the signature engines.
It is independent of \fB\%sign\-verify\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck NAME\fR checks the signature of the summary of a bucket repository with these engines too.
.TP
\fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-key\fR (string)
One trusted public key of the engine \fB\%ENGINE\fR, in base64.
An \fB\%spki\fR key is the base64 of a SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER.
The base64 of an \fB\%spki\fR key can hold line breaks, written as the escape \fB\%\en\fR, and ostrya removes each white space character before the decode.
ostrya refuses an \fB\%spki\fR key that is the base64 of a raw SEC1 point, of the full PEM text, or of a secret key.
.TP
\fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-file\fR (string)
A file of trusted public keys of the engine \fB\%ENGINE\fR.
An \fB\%ed25519\fR file holds one base64 key on each line.
An \fB\%spki\fR file holds PEM \fB\%PUBLIC KEY\fR blocks, and ostrya ignores the text outside the blocks.
ostrya refuses an \fB\%spki\fR file with a block of another label, a block that does not decode, or no block.
An empty file, or a file of white space only, adds no keys.
In an \fB\%spki\fR file, white space is space, horizontal tab, line feed, form feed, and carriage return.
.TP
\fB\%trust\fR (string)
The name of a trust group \fB\%[ex\-ostrya trust "NAME"]\fR.
The keys of the group check the commits of a pull from the remote: \fB\%ostrya pull\fR over each transport, \fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR, and \fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-remote=NAME\fR.
The group alone supplies the keys of the commits: no system key store, no \fB\%<repo>/NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR, no \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR of the remotes directory, and no global trusted keyring.
\fB\%gpg\-verify\-summary\fR, \fB\%sign\-verify\-summary\fR, and the keys that they read still set the check of the summary, and that check still reads these key sources.
A pull refuses a value that is empty, holds \fB\%"\fR or a control character, or names no group.
Beside this key, a pull refuses \fB\%gpg\-verify=true\fR and a \fB\%sign\-verify\fR value other than false, because the group sets the checks of the commits.
\fB\%gpg\-verify=false\fR and an absent \fB\%gpg\-verify\fR give no GPG check of the commits.
A pull also refuses \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR when \fB\%gpg\-verify\-summary\fR is not true, and a \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-key\fR or a \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-file\fR of an engine that \fB\%sign\-verify\-summary\fR does not select.
\fB\%sign\-verify\-summary=true\fR selects \fB\%ed25519\fR and \fB\%spki\fR here.
These refusals read the keys of the config, and a switch of the command does not change them.
\fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-trust=NAME\fR names a trust group for one pull.
The option wins over this key, and the pull then does not read the key.
A switch that turns on a check of the commits is refused.
A switch that turns off a check of the commits turns off that check of the group.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck NAME\fR checks the group and the keys beside this key, and reads no key of the group.
The key is an extension of ostrya, and the \fB\%ostree\fR tool keeps it and ignores it.
.SS "TLS and proxy"
A relative path is relative to the current directory of the command.
ostrya does not expand \fB\%~\fR.
ostrya refuses a TLS file that is larger than 1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-ca\-path\fR (string)
A PEM file of the CA certificates that verify the server, in place of the trust store of the host.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-client\-cert\-path\fR and \fB\%tls\-client\-key\-path\fR (string)
The PEM files of a client certificate and its private key.
Set both or neither.
A key that needs a passphrase is refused.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-permissive\fR (boolean, default false)
A pull accepts each certificate chain, and still checks the host name.
A push to an \fB\%https://\fR address or to an \fB\%s3://\fR address refuses \fB\%true\fR.
.TP
\fB\%proxy\fR (string)
The proxy URL of each request to the remote: \fB\%http://HOST[:PORT]\fR, \fB\%socks5://HOST[:PORT]\fR, or \fB\%socks5h://HOST[:PORT]\fR.
The default port of a SOCKS5 proxy is 1080.
With \fB\%socks5://\fR, ostrya resolves the host name of the remote and sends each address to the proxy in turn.
With \fB\%socks5h://\fR, the proxy resolves the host name of the remote.
A value with another scheme, for example \fB\%socks4://\fR, is refused.
The \fB\%no_proxy\fR variables do not apply to it.
When the key is empty or absent, the proxy variables of the environment apply.
A value with white space at its start or end is refused.
.SS "Push and pull extensions"
These keys are extensions of ostrya.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool keeps them and ignores them.
.TP
\fB\%pull\-url\fR (string)
The pull address: \fB\%ssh://[USER@]HOST[:PORT]/PATH\fR, \fB\%[USER@]HOST:PATH\fR, an \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL, or \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR.
It has precedence over \fB\%url\fR, and \fB\%ostrya pull \-\-url\fR has precedence over it.
A value that starts with \fB\%s3://\fR is the address of a bucket repository of an S3 server.
\fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote refs\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary\fR read the bucket repository at such an address.
\fB\%ostrya remote add\fR writes an \fB\%s3://\fR address to \fB\%pull\-url\fR and \fB\%push\-url\fR, and writes no \fB\%url\fR.
A value that starts with \fB\%ssh://\fR, or that holds no \fB\%://\fR, is an ssh address.
\fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR refuses a \fB\%file:\fR value with no \fB\%//\fR.
For \fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR, a value with the scheme \fB\%file\fR, in letters of either case, is \fB\%file://\fR followed by the absolute path of a local repository.
The command takes the path as written, and refuses a host part (\fB\%localhost\fR too), an empty path, and \fB\%%\fR, \fB\%?\fR, or \fB\%#\fR in the path.
The command ignores the TLS keys, \fB\%proxy\fR, and the ssh keys of such a remote.
Each other pull gives the value to the HTTP client, which refuses a scheme other than \fB\%http\fR and \fB\%https\fR.
A remote that the \fB\%ostree\fR tool also reads must keep an HTTP \fB\%url\fR.
.TP
\fB\%push\-url\fR (string)
The push address, in the forms of \fB\%pull\-url\fR.
If this key is absent and \fB\%url\fR does not start with \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR, a push to the remote fails.
\fB\%ostrya push\fR and \fB\%ostrya push\-tree\fR write the bucket repository at an \fB\%s3://\fR address.
The bucket repository needs a \fB\%config\fR first, which \fB\%ostrya remote init\fR writes.
\fB\%ostrya remote init\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR take the \fB\%s3://\fR address of this key, else the \fB\%s3://\fR address of \fB\%pull\-url\fR.
.TP
\fB\%ssh\-command\fR (string, default ssh)
The ssh command line of a push or a pull over ssh, split at ASCII white space with no quoting rule.
For a push, \fB\%\-\-ssh\-command\fR and \fB\%OSTRYA_SSH_COMMAND\fR have precedence over it.
For a pull, \fB\%OSTRYA_SSH_COMMAND\fR has precedence over it.
.TP
\fB\%receive\-command\fR (string, default ostrya receive)
The command that the remote side runs for a push over ssh.
The remote shell parses it.
\fB\%\-\-receive\-command\fR has precedence over it.
.TP
\fB\%send\-command\fR (string, default ostrya send)
The command that the remote side runs for a pull over ssh.
The remote shell parses it.
.TP
\fB\%push\-token\-file\fR (string)
A file whose first line is the token of a push over HTTP.
A relative path is relative to the current directory of the command, and ostrya does not expand \fB\%~\fR.
The token is token68: letters, digits, \fB\%\-\fR, \fB\%.\fR, \fB\%_\fR, \fB\%~\fR, \fB\%+\fR, and \fB\%/\fR, then zero or more \fB\%=\fR.
A push to an \fB\%http://\fR address refuses to send it, unless the command has \fB\%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR.
A push to an \fB\%s3://\fR address refuses it.
\fB\%\-\-push\-token\-file\fR has precedence over it.
.TP
\fB\%push\-user\fR (string)
The user name of a Basic credential, with the token as the password.
Without it, the push sends the token as a bearer token.
A name that is empty or holds \fB\%:\fR is refused, and so is a name with no token file.
A push to an \fB\%s3://\fR address refuses it.
\fB\%\-\-push\-user\fR has precedence over it.
.TP
\fB\%s3\-region\fR (string, default us\-east\-1)
The region of the signature of each request to an \fB\%s3://\fR address, and the region of the AWS endpoint.
A server that does not check the region needs no value.
A server that checks its own region, and an AWS bucket outside \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR, need it.
.TP
\fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR (string)
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the S3 server, with no path, no query, and no userinfo.
The default is the AWS endpoint of the region.
A command refuses an \fB\%http://\fR endpoint without \fB\%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR, because the signature of each request is a credential.
.TP
\fB\%s3\-addressing\fR (path or virtual)
The addressing style of the bucket: the bucket in the path of each URL, or in the host name.
The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for the AWS endpoint, and \fB\%path\fR for an \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR and for a bucket name that holds a \fB\%.\fR.
Each other value is refused.
.TP
\fB\%s3\-profile\fR (string)
The profile of the shared AWS files of \fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5).
The default is \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR, else \fB\%default\fR.
A profile that this key names turns off the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment.
.PP
The \fB\%ostrya\fR command built without its feature \fB\%s3\fR refuses an \fB\%s3://\fR address, with a message that names the feature.
The options \fB\%\-\-s3\-region\fR, \fB\%\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR, \fB\%\-\-s3\-addressing\fR, and \fB\%\-\-s3\-profile\fR of a command have precedence over the key of the same name.
.PP
A pull and a push refuse an S3 key in a remote whose \fB\%pull\-url\fR and \fB\%push\-url\fR hold no \fB\%s3://\fR address.
So a remote can pull a bucket over its \fB\%https://\fR URL in \fB\%url\fR, and keep an \fB\%s3://\fR address in \fB\%push\-url\fR.
For each S3 setting, the first source that sets it wins: the option, the key, the environment, the profile in the shared config file, and the default.
.PP
The key pair of a pull from an \fB\%s3://\fR address needs the permissions \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR and \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR on the bucket.
Without \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, AWS answers the read of an absent key with 403 \fB\%AccessDenied\fR in place of 404.
The pull reads a 403 as an error, so an absent optional file, for example \fB\%summary.sig\fR or a \fB\%.commitmeta\fR object, then stops the pull.
The \fB\%ostree\fR command pulls a bucket repository with no signature, and reads a 403 for an optional file as an absent file.
\fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5), section BUCKET REPOSITORIES, gives the bucket policy for a pull with the \fB\%ostree\fR command.
.PP
The key pair of a push or a tree push to an \fB\%s3://\fR address needs the permissions \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR, and \fB\%s3:AbortMultipartUpload\fR on the bucket.
The key pair of the init of a bucket repository needs \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, and \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR.
The key pair of \fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR needs the permissions of a push and \fB\%s3:ListBucketMultipartUploads\fR, also with \fB\%\-\-no\-prune\fR.
The key pair of \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR needs \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, and \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR.
Each of these writers takes and removes the writer lock of the bucket repository, which \fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5) describes.
The key pair of \fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR needs \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, and \fB\%s3:ListBucketMultipartUploads\fR.
With \fB\%\-\-delete\fR, it also needs \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR and \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR, and the repair takes and removes the writer lock.
.PP
For an ssh address, a push reads \fB\%ssh\-command\fR and \fB\%receive\-command\fR, and ignores the HTTP keys.
For an HTTP address, a push reads \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR, \fB\%push\-user\fR, \fB\%proxy\fR, and the TLS keys.
For an \fB\%http://\fR address, a push reads no file that a TLS key names, and does not read \fB\%tls\-permissive\fR.
For an \fB\%s3://\fR address, a push, a tree push, \fB\%ostrya remote init\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR read the S3 keys, \fB\%proxy\fR, and the TLS keys.
It refuses \fB\%tls\-permissive=true\fR, \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR, and \fB\%push\-user\fR, and ignores \fB\%ssh\-command\fR, \fB\%send\-command\fR, and \fB\%receive\-command\fR.
.PP
For an ssh address, a pull reads \fB\%ssh\-command\fR and \fB\%send\-command\fR, and ignores \fB\%proxy\fR and the TLS keys.
For an HTTP address, a pull ignores \fB\%ssh\-command\fR and \fB\%send\-command\fR.
For an \fB\%s3://\fR address, a pull reads the S3 keys, \fB\%proxy\fR, and the TLS keys, and ignores \fB\%ssh\-command\fR, \fB\%send\-command\fR, \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR, and \fB\%push\-user\fR.
It signs each request with the key pair of the environment or of the shared AWS files.
.PP
A push or a pull reads each file that it uses one time, before the first request.
If ostrya cannot read a file that a TLS key or \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR names, the error names the key and the path, and holds no byte of the file.
.SH EXTENSION KEYS
The keys of the group \fB\%[ex\-ostrya]\fR.
The group is an extension of ostrya, and the \fB\%ostree\fR tool ignores it.
.TP
\fB\%generate\-composefs\fR (boolean, default false)
Store the composefs image of each complete commit that a write adds and that carries the key \fB\%ostree.composefs.digest.v0\fR.
\fB\%ostrya\-repo\fR(5) describes the image file.
The writers are \fB\%ostrya commit\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR, \fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR (a push, a one\-way stream, and a server commit), and \fB\%ostrya static\-delta apply\-offline\fR.
A pull builds the image of each target, of each parent within the depth, of each dependency, and of each held partial commit that it completes.
A commit\-only pull and a pull with subpaths build no image.
A commit that the repository holds complete before the write gets no image.
\fB\%ostrya composefs\-image \-\-add\fR stores the image of such a commit.
\fB\%ostrya commit\fR and \fB\%ostrya static\-delta apply\-offline\fR also give no image to a commit that the repository holds partial.
With true, \fB\%ostrya commit \-\-generate\-composefs\-metadata\fR also stores the image, as \fB\%\-\-generate\-composefs\fR does.
It stores no image of a commit that the repository holds already.
\fB\%ostrya commit \-\-generate\-composefs\fR stores the image of such a commit, as \fB\%ostrya composefs\-image \-\-add\fR does.
.IP
A malformed key or an image whose fs\-verity digest is not the key fails the write, and the write adds no object and no ref.
For a pull, one such commit fails the whole pull, also when the image of each target is correct.
A later pull of the same commits fetches each object again.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR refuse a malformed key with the code \fB\%protocol\fR, and an image whose digest is not the key with the code \fB\%checksum\-mismatch\fR.
.IP
A value that is not a boolean fails each write before its first write, and a pull before its first request.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR refuse it at the start of a session.
\fB\%ostrya fsck\fR refuses it before its first phase.
ostrya refuses the value true in an \fB\%archive\fR repository in the same places.
.IP
Each image build reads the fs\-verity digest of a sealed object from the kernel.
The build of the images of a transaction reads and hashes each other object one time, and uses that digest for each commit of the transaction and each path that names the object.
With \fB\%[ex\-integrity] fsverity=no\fR, or \fB\%maybe\fR on a file system with no fs\-verity, the build reads and hashes each object of the commits one time.
With true, \fB\%ostrya commit\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR, \fB\%ostrya static\-delta apply\-offline\fR, and \fB\%ostrya receive\fR then write this warning on standard error:
.IP
\fB\%warning: [ex\-ostrya] generate\-composefs is true, but the repository does not seal its objects with fs\-verity; the image build reads and hashes each object with no seal\fR
.IP
\fB\%ostrya pull\fR and \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR write the warning only after a pull that succeeds, and not with \fB\%\-\-commit\-metadata\-only\fR or, for \fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%\-\-subpath\fR.
With \fB\%maybe\fR, the command makes a probe file in \fB\%tmp/\fR to find the result.
The warning does not change the exit status.
The fs\-verity setting applies to the objects that a write adds.
An object that the repository holds from before can have no seal, and each build reads it.
.IP
With true, \fB\%ostrya fsck\fR writes the line \fB\%Commit with no composefs image: COMMIT\fR on standard output for each complete commit with the key and no image.
The line does not change the exit status.
.TP
\fB\%keep\-detached\-related\fR (boolean, default true)
Store the detached metadata key \fB\%ostrya.related\fR that a transfer gives.
With false, \fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR, \fB\%ostrya receive\fR, and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR remove \fB\%ostrya.related\fR from each \fB\%.commitmeta\fR that they write.
A local write ignores the key: \fB\%ostrya commit\fR, \fB\%ostrya sign\fR, and the add of a build source store \fB\%ostrya.related\fR.
A pull, also through \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR and through a static delta, and a push to a bucket repository replace the whole stored \fB\%.commitmeta\fR with the dict that the key leaves.
So a stored \fB\%ostrya.related\fR stays only if that dict is empty: the transfer then writes nothing, and the destination keeps its \fB\%.commitmeta\fR.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR merge the incoming dict into the stored dict, so a stored value stays.
A pull refuses a value that is not a boolean before its first request.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR refuse it at the start of a session.
In the \fB\%config\fR of a bucket repository, \fB\%ostrya push\fR to an \fB\%s3://\fR address reads the key and removes \fB\%ostrya.related\fR from each \fB\%.commitmeta\fR that it uploads.
\fB\%ostrya push\fR, \fB\%ostrya push\-tree\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR refuse a bucket repository whose value is not a boolean, and \fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR reports it as a \fB\%config\fR error.
.TP
\fB\%remotes\-config\-dir\fR (string)
The remotes directory of the repository: a directory of \fB\%.conf\fR files that hold remotes.
A create of a repository through the ostrya library with a remotes directory writes the key into the new \fB\%config\fR.
A path value gives the absolute form of the path, with no symlink resolution.
An in-repository value gives its relative path as given.
A descriptor value gives no key.
\fB\%ostrya init \-\-remotes\-config\-dir=PATH\fR writes the key with the absolute form of PATH.
Each open of the repository reads the key.
The option \fB\%\-\-remotes\-config\-dir\fR of the \fB\%ostrya\fR command comes before the key, and \fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d\fR for the system repository comes after it.
A remotes directory that a program gives through the ostrya library comes before the key, and a default of the program comes after it.
An absolute value is a path.
A relative value is relative to the repository directory.
ostrya refuses an empty value at each open, also when a program gives a remotes directory.
\fB\%ostrya config set\fR writes an empty value with no check.
The repository then does not open, so no \fB\%ostrya\fR command can change the value, \fB\%ostrya config set\fR and \fB\%ostrya config unset\fR included.
Edit \fB\%config\fR by hand to correct the value.
A change of the key takes effect at the next open.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool ignores the key.
"Remotes directory" under REMOTE KEYS gives the rules of the read.
.SH CLIENT AND SERVER GROUPS
Client groups control the access to a repository through \fB\%ostrya serve\fR, \fB\%ostrya receive\fR, and \fB\%ostrya send\fR.
A client group states how a client authenticates, which refs it can read and change in each namespace, how the server verifies its commits, and which keys sign them.
These groups are extensions of ostrya, and the \fB\%ostree\fR tool ignores them:
.TP
\fB\%[ex\-ostrya server]\fR
The settings of \fB\%ostrya serve\fR.
.TP
\fB\%[ex\-ostrya client "NAME"]\fR
One client group.
.TP
\fB\%[ex\-ostrya trust "NAME"]\fR
A set of trusted keys.
.TP
\fB\%[ex\-ostrya key "NAME"]\fR
A signing key of the server.
.PP
ostrya refuses an unknown key, a reference to a group that does not exist, and a group name that starts with \fB\%ex\-ostrya \fR and has no form of this list.
It refuses \fB\%[ex\-ostrya receive]\fR and \fB\%[ex\-ostrya receive "PATTERN"]\fR, with a message that names \fB\%[ex\-ostrya client "NAME"]\fR.
It refuses the key \fB\%collection\fR of a client group, with a message that names \fB\%@ID:PATTERN\fR.
The quoted part of a group name is not empty and holds no \fB\%"\fR and no control character.
A repeated key takes the last value, so each list key is one line in a group.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR and \fB\%ostrya send\fR without \fB\%\-\-one\-way\fR, and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR, read and check the client groups and the server group at start.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR without \fB\%\-\-one\-way\fR and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR also build each trust group and each key group one time, also a group that no client group names.
Thus a key source that ostrya cannot read stops the command.
\fB\%ostrya send\fR reads no key of a trust group or a key group.
\fB\%ostrya receive \-\-one\-way\fR and \fB\%ostrya send \-\-one\-way\fR read no client group, no server group, and no key group.
A pull from a remote with the key \fB\%trust\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-trust=NAME\fR, and \fB\%ostrya receive \-\-one\-way \-\-trust=NAME\fR check and build the one trust group that they name, and no other group.
A bad group stops the command with status 1.
A push session names its namespace in \fB\%Hello\fR: the local refs, or one collection.
The \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR names the local refs.
\fB\%ostrya receive\fR gives a session the client group that holds the \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR item of the client name.
With no such group, the session gets \fB\%ref\-denied\fR.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR gives a session the client group that holds the item that identified the request.
With no such group, the request gets 403.
The session takes the allow items and the deny items of the group for its namespace.
A session of a collection reads and writes the refs below \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR alone.
\fB\%ostrya send\fR serves the refs that the group of the \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR item reads.
With client groups and no group that holds that item, it writes an error line and exits 1 before it reads its input.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR takes its settings from the server group, and an option of the command has precedence over the key of its name.
.SS "Server keys"
The keys of \fB\%[ex\-ostrya server]\fR.
An option of \fB\%ostrya serve\fR with the name of a key has precedence over the key.
A relative path in a key is relative to the repository directory, and a relative path in an option is relative to the current directory.
An empty value of \fB\%listen\fR, \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR, \fB\%sign\-summary\fR, \fB\%tls\-cert\fR, \fB\%tls\-key\fR, or \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR is refused.
To get the default, remove the key.
.TP
\fB\%listen\fR (list of \fB\%ADDR:PORT\fR, default \fB\%127.0.0.1:8080\fR)
The addresses to listen on.
A host name is refused.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-cert\fR (string)
The PEM certificate chain.
It needs \fB\%tls\-key\fR.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-key\fR (string)
The PEM private key.
It needs \fB\%tls\-cert\fR.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR (string)
The PEM CA certificates that verify a client certificate.
It needs \fB\%tls\-cert\fR or \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR.
A server with neither of them ignores the key, so a plain HTTP server can use the config of an HTTPS server.
The server verifies the certificate of the TLS handshake and the certificate that a proxy forwards with these certificates.
.TP
\fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR (list of CIDR)
The peers whose forwarding data the server reads.
It needs \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-header\fR.
From such a peer, the server reads the client address from the header of \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-header\fR, and walks the hops of the header from the right.
It skips each hop in a trusted network, and the first other hop is the client address.
When all hops are trusted, the client is the leftmost hop.
A hop with no address, for example \fB\%unknown\fR, gives a client with no address, which matches no \fB\%net:\fR item.
A header that is absent, or that has one line of white space alone, keeps the peer address.
A header with other text and no hop, for example an \fB\%X\-Forwarded\-For\fR value of commas alone, gives no address.
A trusted proxy must set or remove the forwarding header, \fB\%X\-Forwarded\-Proto\fR, and the header of \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR in each request.
From a peer that is not trusted, the server reads none of these headers.
.TP
\fB\%trusted\-proxy\-header\fR (\fB\%forwarded\fR, \fB\%x\-forwarded\-for\fR, or \fB\%x\-real\-ip\fR)
The one request header from which the server reads the client address of a request from a trusted proxy: \fB\%Forwarded\fR of RFC 7239, \fB\%X\-Forwarded\-For\fR, or \fB\%X\-Real\-IP\fR.
It needs \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR.
A hop of \fB\%Forwarded\fR is the \fB\%for\fR value of one element.
A hop of the other two headers is \fB\%ADDR\fR, \fB\%IPV4:PORT\fR, or \fB\%[IPV6]:PORT\fR.
The request counts as TLS when the trusted proxy reports \fB\%https\fR, in any case: with \fB\%forwarded\fR, in the \fB\%proto\fR of the client hop, and with the other two, in the rightmost value of \fB\%X\-Forwarded\-Proto\fR.
.TP
\fB\%trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR (string)
The name of the request header that carries the client certificate from a trusted proxy.
The name is one or more ASCII letters, digits, and the characters \fB\%!#$%&'*+\-.^_`|~\fR.
It needs \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR and \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR.
The server percent\-decodes the value.
The value is then a PEM certificate, as nginx \fB\%$ssl_client_escaped_cert\fR gives it, or a base64 DER certificate, as HAProxy \fB\%ssl_c_der,base64\fR and Traefik \fB\%passTLSClientCert\fR give it.
An empty value is no certificate.
A value with more than one certificate, for example with a comma, a value that does not parse, and a certificate that \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR does not verify get 403.
The server uses no intermediate certificate from the proxy, so \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR must hold each intermediate CA.
From a trusted proxy, this header alone gives the certificate, and the server does not use the certificate of the TLS handshake.
A request with no such header gives no certificate.
.TP
\fB\%proxy\-protocol\fR (boolean, default false)
Each connection starts with a PROXY protocol v1 or v2 header, before the TLS handshake.
The source address of the header is the client address.
A header with no address, for example \fB\%UNKNOWN\fR, keeps the peer address.
The server closes a connection with no valid header within 30 seconds, and sends no response.
Each peer that can connect states its own client address in the header, so only the proxy must be able to reach the listener.
The check of \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR uses the source address of the header, so the server does not read the forwarding headers and the forwarded certificate of a proxy that states the address of its client in the PROXY header.
.TP
\fB\%allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR (boolean, default false)
Take a bearer or Basic credential on a connection with no TLS.
A request from a trusted proxy that reports \fB\%https\fR counts as TLS.
.TP
\fB\%session\-timeout\fR (integer, 1 or more)
The idle time of a push session, in seconds.
.TP
\fB\%max\-sessions\fR (integer, 1 or more)
The number of push sessions at the same time.
.TP
\fB\%parallel\-uploads\fR (integer, 1 to 31)
The number of object streams of a push session at the same time.
.TP
\fB\%body\-timeout\fR (integer, 1 or more)
The time in seconds that a response waits for the client to take its next bytes.
.TP
\fB\%sign\-summary\fR (list)
The key groups whose keys sign each summary that a push writes again.
A name that occurs two times counts one time.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR has no option for the key, because \fB\%ostrya receive\fR reads it too.
.PP
A CIDR value, in \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR and in a \fB\%net:\fR item, is \fB\%ADDR/LEN\fR or a bare \fB\%ADDR\fR.
A bare IPv4 address is \fB\%ADDR/32\fR, and a bare IPv6 address is \fB\%ADDR/128\fR.
\fB\%LEN\fR has decimal digits alone, with no sign and no leading zero.
A value with host bits set, for example \fB\%10.0.0.1/24\fR, is refused.
An IPv6 address with a zone id is refused.
An IPv4\-mapped IPv6 address is refused, and the IPv4 form names the same network.
.SS "Client keys"
The keys of \fB\%[ex\-ostrya client "NAME"]\fR.
.TP
\fB\%auth\fR (list)
The authentication items of the group.
The key is necessary.
An item that occurs two times counts one time.
.TP
\fB\%allow\fR (list)
The allow items, each \fB\%[MODES:]PATTERN\fR for the local refs or \fB\%[MODES:]@ID:PATTERN\fR for the collection \fB\%ID\fR (see "Access modes and patterns").
Without the key, the group has no right on a ref.
.TP
\fB\%deny\fR (list)
The deny items, each \fB\%PATTERN\fR for the local refs or \fB\%@ID:PATTERN\fR for the collection \fB\%ID\fR.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-accept\-claim\fR (boolean, default false)
Read the claims of the client certificate (see "Claims").
The claims of a namespace replace the allow items and the deny items of the group for that namespace.
A group with no \fB\%tls:\fR or \fB\%tls\-sha256:\fR item refuses the key, with any value.
.TP
\fB\%verify\fR (string)
\fB\%trust:NAME\fR, the trust group that verifies each new commit of a ref update that a client builds and pushes.
The trust group verifies no other commit: no staged parent and no dependency in field 2 of a commit, unless a ref update also takes that commit as its new commit.
The checksum of a dependency is in field 2 of a commit.
A new commit is that commit, or binds it through the parent checksums of its history, and the signatures of the new commit cover these checksums.
Each other value is refused, \fB\%off\fR and \fB\%remote:NAME\fR included.
Without the key, the group verifies no signature.
.TP
\fB\%allow\-unsigned\fR (boolean)
Accept a new commit of a ref update that a client builds and pushes, when it has no signature.
A commit has no signature if its detached metadata holds no blob under \fB\%ostree.gpgsigs\fR, \fB\%ostree.sign.ed25519\fR, \fB\%ostree.sign.spki\fR, and \fB\%ostree.sign.dummy\fR.
A commit with a signature must verify on each axis of the trust group, also when the key is true, so a signature of an engine that the trust group does not read is refused.
The default is true without \fB\%verify\fR, and false with \fB\%verify\fR.
\fB\%allow\-unsigned=false\fR needs \fB\%verify\fR.
.TP
\fB\%sign\fR (list)
The key groups whose keys sign each new commit of a ref update of the group, and each server commit of the group.
The keys sign no other commit: no staged parent and no dependency in field 2 of a commit, unless a ref update also takes that commit as its new commit.
A key that already signed a commit adds no signature.
A name that occurs two times counts one time.
An empty value is refused.
.TP
\fB\%allow\-privileged\fR (boolean, default false)
In a \fB\%bare\fR repository, accept the setuid and setgid bits, and the extended attributes \fB\%security.capability\fR and \fB\%security.selinux\fR.
The rule applies to each content object and each dirmeta object that a session receives, also to an object that the repository already holds.
A session on a \fB\%bare\fR repository needs a server that runs as root.
.TP
\fB\%summary\fR (boolean, default false)
Give each authentication item of the group a summary (see "Summaries").
A group with \fB\%tls\-accept\-claim=true\fR refuses \fB\%summary=true\fR.
.SS "Authentication items"
\fB\%auth\fR is a list.
A group matches when one of its items matches.
.TP
\fB\%anonymous\fR
A request with no credential and no client address match.
.TP
\fB\%tls:*\fR
A client certificate that \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR verified.
.TP
\fB\%tls:NAME\fR
A verified client certificate whose subject holds exactly one CN, of type UTF8String or PrintableString, equal to \fB\%NAME\fR byte for byte.
.TP
\fB\%tls\-sha256:HEX\fR
A verified client certificate whose DER bytes have the SHA\-256 digest \fB\%HEX\fR.
.TP
\fB\%token\-sha256:HEX\fR
A bearer token whose SHA\-256 digest is \fB\%HEX\fR.
The digest covers the whole token.
.TP
\fB\%basic\-sha256:USER:HEX\fR
A Basic credential whose user name is \fB\%USER\fR and whose password has the SHA\-256 digest \fB\%HEX\fR.
The item splits at the last \fB\%:\fR.
\fB\%USER\fR is not empty and holds no \fB\%:\fR and no control character.
.TP
\fB\%ssh:NAME\fR
An ssh session whose client name is \fB\%NAME\fR.
.TP
\fB\%net:CIDR\fR
A request whose client address is in \fB\%CIDR\fR.
.PP
\fB\%HEX\fR is 64 lowercase hex digits.
\fB\%NAME\fR and \fB\%USER\fR do not start or end with white space.
On a server with no \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR, the \fB\%tls:\fR and \fB\%tls\-sha256:\fR items match no request, and the other items of their groups still match.
Two items are the same when their values are equal after the parse, so \fB\%net:2001:0db8::/56\fR and \fB\%net:2001:db8::/56\fR are the same item.
.SS "Access modes and patterns"
An allow item is \fB\%[MODES:]PATTERN\fR for the local refs, or \fB\%[MODES:]@ID:PATTERN\fR for the refs of the collection \fB\%ID\fR.
A deny item is \fB\%PATTERN\fR or \fB\%@ID:PATTERN\fR.
\fB\%MODES\fR comes before \fB\%@ID:\fR.
\fB\%ID\fR is a literal collection id: it passes the collection\-id rule and is at most 255 bytes.
The item splits at the first \fB\%:\fR after the \fB\%@\fR.
No item names more than one collection.
\fB\%@ID:PATTERN\fR with the \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR is the same as \fB\%PATTERN\fR.
The items of one namespace give no right in another namespace.
.PP
\fB\%MODES\fR is one or more of these letters, each at most one time, in any order, and an item with no \fB\%MODES\fR has the mode \fB\%r\fR:
.TP
\fB\%r\fR
Read the ref.
.TP
\fB\%w\fR
Write a commit that the client built to the ref, as a fast\-forward.
.TP
\fB\%c\fR
Let the server make a commit on the branch.
.TP
\fB\%t\fR
Create the ref as a tag.
An update with \fB\%t\fR and no \fB\%w\fR creates an absent ref, and gets \fB\%ref\-mismatch\fR for a ref that exists, also with the current commit.
.TP
\fB\%d\fR
Delete the ref.
.PP
\fB\%w\fR, \fB\%c\fR, and \fB\%t\fR add \fB\%r\fR, and \fB\%d\fR does not.
\fB\%wd\fR allows a push that is not a fast\-forward, when the client sends \fB\%\-\-force\fR.
\fB\%cd\fR allows a server commit with no parent on a branch that exists.
.PP
\fB\%PATTERN\fR is a series of parts split at \fB\%/\fR.
Each part is a literal, \fB\%*\fR for exactly one part, or \fB\%**\fR for one or more parts.
For example, \fB\%apps/*\fR matches \fB\%apps/web\fR and does not match \fB\%apps/web/db\fR.
\fB\%apps/**\fR matches both, and neither pattern matches \fB\%apps\fR.
A pattern has no remote part.
.PP
The effective rights of a ref are the union of the modes of each allow item of its namespace that matches the ref.
When a deny item of its namespace matches the ref, the ref has no mode.
A push session follows these rules:
.IP \(bu 2
A group opens a push session only when an allow item of any namespace has \fB\%w\fR, \fB\%c\fR, \fB\%t\fR, or \fB\%d\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Each ref of the session needs \fB\%r\fR in the namespace of the session.
So a delete needs \fB\%r\fR and \fB\%d\fR, for example the item \fB\%rd:PATTERN\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
An update that writes a commit needs \fB\%w\fR or \fB\%t\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A server commit needs \fB\%c\fR on its branch.
A server commit with no parent on a branch that exists needs \fB\%cd\fR, else it is \fB\%non\-fast\-forward\fR.
Each tag of a server commit needs \fB\%t\fR or \fB\%w\fR, and a tag that exists gets \fB\%ref\-mismatch\fR, also with \fB\%w\fR.
A bound name of a server commit gets no ref and needs no mode.
\fB\%verify\fR and \fB\%allow\-unsigned\fR do not apply to a server commit.
.IP \(bu 2
A push writes no remote ref, and no \fB\%ostree\-metadata\fR in the local refs of a repository with a collection id.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fB\%ostree.collection\-binding\fR of a new commit that the client built must name the collection of the namespace.
For the local refs, that is the \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR, and in a repository with no collection id, each collection binding is refused.
A commit with no collection binding passes in each namespace.
.IP \(bu 2
The parent chain of a new branch can stop at any depth.
On a branch that exists, the chain of the new commit must reach the current commit, else the update is \fB\%non\-fast\-forward\fR.
.SS "Claims"
A claim is a URI SAN of a client certificate.
A group with \fB\%tls\-accept\-claim=true\fR reads the claims of the certificate that gives its \fB\%tls:\fR or \fB\%tls\-sha256:\fR item.
A claim has one of these forms:
.TP
\fB\%ostrya:ref:[MODES:]PATTERN\fR
An allow item of the local refs.
.TP
\fB\%ostrya:ref:!PATTERN\fR
A deny item of the local refs.
.TP
\fB\%ostrya:ref:[MODES:]@ID:PATTERN\fR
An allow item of the collection \fB\%ID\fR.
.TP
\fB\%ostrya:ref:!@ID:PATTERN\fR
A deny item of the collection \fB\%ID\fR.
.PP
The text after \fB\%ostrya:ref:\fR is an allow item of "Access modes and patterns", or \fB\%!\fR and a deny item, and a claim with no \fB\%MODES\fR has the mode \fB\%r\fR.
\fB\%MODES\fR comes before \fB\%@ID:\fR, and \fB\%!\fR comes before \fB\%@ID:\fR.
A claim with \fB\%@ID:\fR of the \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR is a claim of the local refs.
.IP \(bu 2
When the certificate holds one or more claims for a namespace, they replace the allow items and the deny items of the group for that namespace.
In each other namespace, the items of the group apply.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%verify\fR, \fB\%allow\-unsigned\fR, \fB\%sign\fR, and \fB\%allow\-privileged\fR come from the group.
.IP \(bu 2
A URI whose scheme is not \fB\%ostrya\fR, in any case, has no effect.
.IP \(bu 2
A URI whose scheme is \fB\%ostrya\fR, in any case, and that is no claim refuses the request with 403.
A claim has the one kind \fB\%ref\fR, and the scheme and the kind are lowercase.
So a URI that starts with \fB\%ostrya:collection:\fR refuses the request.
More than 256 such URIs also refuse the request.
So do a certificate that does not decode, a certificate with more than one subjectAltName extension, and a subjectAltName extension that does not decode.
Each case has its own reason.
The server writes an error to standard error that names the SHA\-256 digest of the certificate and the URI.
.PP
The claims can give more rights than the group, so the CA of \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR grants the rights of each certificate that it signs.
A credential, a \fB\%net:\fR item, and the \fB\%anonymous\fR item take no claims.
.SS "Read access"
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR identifies the client of each read of a file and of the ref listing, as it identifies a push.
A read that identifies no item gets 401 or 403 and no file.
\fB\%ostrya send\fR identifies the client of a session by the \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR item of the client name.
The group of the item then gives the read rights:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%refs/heads/NAME\fR is in the local refs, and \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/NAME\fR is in the collection \fB\%ID\fR.
When \fB\%ID\fR is the \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR, the path is in the local refs, and the items of the local refs give the rights of \fB\%NAME\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
The server serves such a ref when the group of the item has \fB\%r\fR on \fB\%NAME\fR in its namespace.
An alias needs \fB\%r\fR on its name and on its target.
.IP \(bu 2
The ref listing holds the local refs that the client reads.
A request that names the collection refs gets them too: each collection takes the items of the group for that collection.
The items of two namespaces never combine.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory under \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR whose name is not a collection id gives no right.
Its refs are not served, and the ref listing leaves out the directory.
The server writes one warning that names the directory.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%refs/remotes/\fR, each other path under \fB\%refs/\fR, and \fB\%extensions/ostrya/\fR are not served.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR are the files of the summary of the item (see "Summaries").
.IP \(bu 2
Objects, deltas, \fB\%config\fR, and the other files under \fB\%extensions/\fR are served to each identified client.
.PP
A path that the client cannot read gets the answer of an absent path: 404 over HTTP, and no file over ssh.
With \fB\%tls\-accept\-claim=true\fR, the claims of the certificate set the read rights too, and claims that do not parse refuse each read with 403.
.SS "Summaries"
An authentication item has a summary when its group has \fB\%summary=true\fR.
Each other item has no summary.
The summary of an item holds the refs that the item reads in each namespace.
The claims of a client certificate do not apply.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR writes the summary of each item under \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR, \fB\%anonymous\fR included (see \fBostrya\-repo\fR(5)).
It also writes \fB\%summary\fR in the repository root, which holds each ref, whatever the client groups.
The command also writes the access digest of each item: a digest of \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR, and of the allow items, the deny items, and \fB\%summary\fR of the group of the item in each namespace.
So the digest changes when a change to the config can change the refs of the summary.
The group names are not in the digest.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u \-c\fR writes the summary of each item alone, and leaves \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR in the repository root as they are.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR and \fB\%ostrya send\fR serve \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR of the identified item, and never the files in the repository root.
An item with no summary gets the answer of an absent path.
When a change to the config changes the access digest of an item, the summary of the item gets the answer of an absent path until \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR, and the server writes one error that names the item.
.SS "No client group"
A repository with no client group has one group, \fB\%anonymous\fR.
It holds the item \fB\%anonymous\fR, has \fB\%summary=true\fR, and gives \fB\%r\fR on each local ref and on each ref of each collection.
No value of \fB\%allow\fR names each collection, so a client group cannot state these rights.
.PP
So each client reads each local ref and each mirror ref, and gets the summary of \fB\%anonymous\fR, which holds each ref.
No client opens a push session.
An ssh session of \fB\%ostrya send\fR gets the \fB\%anonymous\fR item in such a repository, whatever its client name, so this group applies to it.
An ssh session of \fB\%ostrya receive\fR uses no \fB\%anonymous\fR item.
.SS "Start checks"
ostrya refuses the config in these cases:
.IP \(bu 2
Two groups carry the same item.
.IP \(bu 2
A group has the key \fB\%collection\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A group has \fB\%tls\-accept\-claim\fR and no \fB\%tls:\fR or \fB\%tls\-sha256:\fR item.
.IP \(bu 2
A group has \fB\%tls\-accept\-claim=true\fR and \fB\%summary=true\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A group has \fB\%allow\-unsigned=false\fR and no \fB\%verify\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fB\%ID\fR of an item \fB\%@ID:PATTERN\fR fails the collection\-id rule or is longer than 255 bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fB\%verify\fR, \fB\%sign\fR, or \fB\%sign\-summary\fR value names a trust group or a key group that does not exist.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR also refuses to start in these cases.
It runs these checks after each option replaces the key of its name:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR is set, and the server has no \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR or no \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR is set, and the server has no \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-header\fR, or the reverse.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%tls\-cert\fR is set and \fB\%tls\-key\fR is not, or the reverse.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%\-\-tls\-client\-ca\fR is given, and \fB\%tls\-cert\fR and \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR are not set.
.SS "Trust keys"
A trust group takes the remote keys \fB\%gpg\-verify\fR (default false here), \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR, \fB\%sign\-verify\fR, \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-key\fR, and \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-file\fR.
A \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-key\fR and a \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-file\fR have the form of the remote keys.
An \fB\%spki\fR key is the base64 of a SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER, and it can hold line breaks.
A \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-file\fR holds base64 lines for \fB\%ed25519\fR, and PEM \fB\%PUBLIC KEY\fR blocks for \fB\%spki\fR.
It trusts the keys that it names, and no system key store or keyring.
A trust group that turns on no check is refused.
\fB\%gpgkeypath\fR without \fB\%gpg\-verify=true\fR is refused, and so is \fB\%gpg\-verify=true\fR with no \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR.
ostrya reads \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR as it reads the remote key: it reads the raw value, decodes no escape sequence, and splits the value at each \fB\%;\fR.
One \fB\%;\fR at the end of the value gives no entry.
Each other empty entry of \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR is refused when the config is read, and so is an empty value.
An entry of \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR that names neither a file nor a directory is refused, and so are keyrings that hold no certificate.
A \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-key\fR or \fB\%verification\-ENGINE\-file\fR key of an engine that \fB\%sign\-verify\fR does not select is refused.
\fB\%gpg\-verify\-summary\fR and \fB\%sign\-verify\-summary\fR are refused, because a trust group sets a commit policy alone.
A trust group checks the new commits of a push that a client group with \fB\%verify=trust:NAME\fR accepts, the commits of a pull from a remote with \fB\%trust=NAME\fR, and the commits of \fB\%ostrya pull\-local \-\-trust=NAME\fR.
Thus the keys that \fB\%ostrya remote gpg\-import NAME\fR writes to \fB\%<repo>/NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR check no commit of a pull with a trust group, from the key \fB\%trust\fR or from \fB\%\-\-trust\fR.
The GPG check of the summary of the remote \fB\%NAME\fR still reads them.
The option \fB\%\-\-trust\fR wins over the key \fB\%trust\fR of the remote that \fB\%\-\-remote\fR names, and needs no \fB\%\-\-remote\fR.
With \fB\%\-\-remote\fR, the refusals of the remote keys beside \fB\%trust\fR apply to that remote, and its summary keys set the check of the summary.
Without \fB\%\-\-remote\fR, the pull checks no summary.
.SS "Signing key keys"
A key group refuses a key of another type, for example \fB\%gpg\-key\fR in an \fB\%ed25519\fR group.
.TP
\fB\%type\fR (string)
\fB\%ed25519\fR, \fB\%spki\fR, or \fB\%gpg\fR.
The key is necessary.
\fB\%dummy\fR is refused.
.TP
\fB\%secret\-key\-file\fR (string)
For \fB\%ed25519\fR and \fB\%spki\fR: a regular file of at most 1 MiB.
For \fB\%ed25519\fR, the file holds exactly one line that is not blank, the base64 of the 64\-byte seed and public key.
For \fB\%spki\fR, the file holds PEM \fB\%PRIVATE KEY\fR blocks, each a PKCS#8 DER of a P\-256, a P\-384, an Ed25519, or an RSA key, as \fB\%ostrya sign \-\-keys\-file\fR takes them.
Each block gives one signing key, in the order of the file.
An empty file, a file with no block, a block with another label, and a block that holds no valid PKCS#8 key are refused.
The time of an RSA signature can disclose information about the private key, and a client that pushes can start a signature and measure its time.
Where a client that is not trusted can push, use a P\-256, a P\-384, or an Ed25519 key in an \fB\%spki\fR group, or an \fB\%ed25519\fR group.
.TP
\fB\%gpg\-key\fR (string)
For \fB\%gpg\fR: a selector that names exactly one secret key.
.TP
\fB\%gpg\-homedir\fR (string)
For \fB\%gpg\fR: the GnuPG home directory.
It is optional.
.SH KEYS WITHOUT EFFECT
ostrya keeps these keys and does not use them:
.TP
\fB\%[core] disable\-xattrs\fR, \fB\%[core] parent\fR, \fB\%[core] default\-repo\-finders\fR
ostrya does not read these keys.
The default of \fB\%default\-repo\-finders\fR is \fB\%config;mount\fR.
.TP
\fB\%contenturl\fR, \fB\%metalink\fR, and \fB\%custom\-backend\fR of a remote
\fB\%ostrya remote add\fR writes them, and a pull does not read them.
.PP
ostrya reads and writes the remote groups of a remotes directory only when the repository has one.
The \fB\%ostrya\fR command finds the directory in the order of "Remotes directory" under REMOTE KEYS: the option \fB\%\-\-remotes\-config\-dir\fR, the key \fB\%remotes\-config\-dir\fR of \fB\%[ex\-ostrya]\fR, then \fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d\fR for the system repository.
A program that uses the ostrya library can also give the directory when it opens the repository.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fB\%OSTRYA_SSH_COMMAND\fR
The ssh command line.
It has precedence over the \fB\%ssh\-command\fR key.
.TP
\fB\%AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fR, \fB\%AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fR, \fB\%AWS_SESSION_TOKEN\fR
The key pair that signs each request to an \fB\%s3://\fR address, and its session token.
They have precedence over the shared AWS files.
.TP
\fB\%AWS_REGION\fR, \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR
The region of an \fB\%s3://\fR address, if \fB\%s3\-region\fR is absent.
\fB\%AWS_REGION\fR has precedence.
.TP
\fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR, \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR
The endpoint of an \fB\%s3://\fR address, if \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR is absent.
\fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR has precedence.
.TP
\fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files, if \fB\%s3\-profile\fR is absent.
.TP
\fB\%AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE\fR, \fB\%AWS_CONFIG_FILE\fR
The paths of the shared AWS credentials file and config file.
.PP
A profile that \fB\%s3\-profile\fR or \fB\%\-\-s3\-profile\fR names turns off \fB\%AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fR, \fB\%AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fR, \fB\%AWS_SESSION_TOKEN\fR, \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR, \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR, and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR.
.PP
The commands that read and write a bucket repository at an \fB\%s3://\fR address read the AWS variables: \fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya push\fR, \fB\%ostrya push\-tree\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote init\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote refs\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary\fR.
The \fB\%ostrya\fR command built without its feature \fB\%s3\fR refuses an \fB\%s3://\fR address, and does not read them.
.TP
\fB\%http_proxy\fR, \fB\%https_proxy\fR, \fB\%HTTPS_PROXY\fR, \fB\%all_proxy\fR, \fB\%ALL_PROXY\fR, \fB\%no_proxy\fR, \fB\%NO_PROXY\fR
The proxy of a remote whose \fB\%proxy\fR key is empty or absent.
ostrya does not read \fB\%HTTP_PROXY\fR.
.TP
\fB\%OSTREE_GPG_HOME\fR
The directory of the global trusted GPG keyrings, in place of \fB\%/usr/share/ostree/trusted.gpg.d\fR.
A value that names a file that is not a directory is refused when ostrya reads the global keyrings.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the mode of the repository:
.PP
.nf
ostrya \-\-repo=repo config get core.mode
.fi
.PP
Read a key of a remote:
.PP
.nf
ostrya \-\-repo=repo config \-\-group='remote "origin"' get url
.fi
.PP
A remote that pulls over HTTP and pushes over ssh:
.PP
.nf
[remote "origin"]
url=https://ostree.example.com/repo
push\-url=ssh://builder@ostree.example.com/srv/repo
gpg\-verify=false
sign\-verify=ed25519
verification\-ed25519\-file=/etc/ostrya/origin.ed25519
.fi
.PP
A client group that reads each ref and pushes the refs below \fB\%apps/\fR with a trusted signature:
.PP
.nf
[ex\-ostrya client "builder"]
auth=ssh:builder
allow=r:**;w:apps/**
verify=trust:release
[ex\-ostrya trust "release"]
sign\-verify=ed25519
verification\-ed25519\-file=/etc/ostrya/release.ed25519
.fi
.PP
A client group that reads each local ref, mirrors the refs below \fB\%os/\fR of the collection \fB\%com.example.Os\fR, and cannot read \fB\%os/secret\fR of that collection:
.PP
.nf
[ex\-ostrya client "mirror"]
auth=tls:mirror.example.com
allow=r:**;w:@com.example.Os:os/**
deny=@com.example.Os:os/secret
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
